        "title": "Randomized controlled trial of a modular, transdiagnostic, cognitive-behavioral anxiety intervention versus primary care behavioral health treatment as usual.",
        "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42413627/",
        "layer": "research_frontier",
        "status": "active",
        "section_path": "abstract/0",
        "section_heading": "Abstract",
        "ordinal": 1,
        "text": "Post hoc analyses suggested baseline depression severity moderated the condition effect; participants with moderate and severe comorbid depression responded better to MAST than PCBH TAU. While MAST was not superior to PCBH TAU at post-treatment as hypothesized, it may potentially yield a more enduring impact and work better with comorbid depression; however, these findings require prospective replication. This study advances the literature by comparing brief CBT for anxiety in primary care to a strong active comparison comprising behavioral treatment. MAST may offer implementation advantages due to being manualized and modular. Further research on modular CBT for anxiety is warranted.",
        "text_hash": "dcddb7e5a3030ea4f85721db8ad4a049c00727b59571595d1812f0293ad5bd3c",
